%I A400665 #15 Oct 08 2026 23:41:18
%S A400665 1,1,2,3,4,5,6,11,16,21,26,31,57,83,109,135,244,353,462,571,680,1251,
%T A400665 1822,2393,2964,5357,8321,11285,14249,17213,20177,23141,26105,29069,
%U A400665 32033,34997,37961,40925,43889,46853,49817,52781,55745,58709,61673,64637,67601,70565
%N A400665 Denominators of semiconvergents to log_2(7), which equals log(7)/log(2).
%C A400665 Here semiconvergents include principal convergents, and the initial term 1 occurs twice. Let alpha = log_2(7) and delta_r = r*alpha - floor(r*alpha). The remaining terms are precisely the indices r at which delta_r attains a strict record minimum or maximum. These are the lower and upper equality orders, respectively, for the bounds on A174796(r). At these orders, A174796(r) is a rational Catalan number. This sequence is the log_2(7) analog of A400205. - _Mike Winkler_, Oct 03 2026
%H A400665 Mike Winkler, <a href="/A400665/b400665.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..200</a>
%H A400665 Mike Winkler, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.22303">Admissible qx+1 Sequences, Semiconvergents, and Rational Catalan Numbers</a>, arXiv:2609.22303 [math.GM], Sep 2026.
%F A400665 Let alpha=[a_0;a_1,a_2,...], Q_(-1)=0, Q_0=1, and Q_(k+1)=a_(k+1)*Q_k+Q_(k-1). This sequence is the increasing rearrangement, with multiplicity, of s*Q_k+Q_(k-1) for k>=0 and 0<=s<a_(k+1), with 0 deleted. The lower-side terms arise for odd k>=1 and the upper-side terms for even k>=0, excluding (k,s)=(0,0).
%e A400665 Lower-side terms begin: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 31, 57, 83, 109, 680, 1251, 1822, 2393, 5357, 8321, 11285, 14249.
%e A400665 Upper-side terms begin: 1, 6, 11, 16, 21, 26, 135, 244, 353, 462, 571, 2964, 91313, 544914, 998515.
%o A400665 (PARI) semidens(n,q=7) = {my(c=contfrac(log(q)/log(2),n), qm=0, Q=1, t, v=List()); for(i=1,#c-1, for(s=0,c[i+1]-1, t=s*Q+qm; if(t>0,listput(v,t))); t=c[i+1]*Q+qm; qm=Q; Q=t); vecsort(Vec(v))}
%Y A400665 Cf. A028509 (continued fraction expansion of log_2(7)), A174796 (7x+1 admissible-sequence counts), A206788 (the analogous sequence for log_2(3)), A400205 (the analogous sequence for log_2(5)), A006013.
%K A400665 nonn,frac,easy,new
%O A400665 1,3
%A A400665 _Mike Winkler_, Oct 03 2026
